ï»¿    **COMMENTS**
The 6 April 2009 L'Aquila earthquake (Mw 6.3) struck a portion of the Central Apennines that was believed to be especially well known from a seismotectonic point of view. The region is undergoing SW-NE active extension and is affected by strong earthquakes with magnitude up to 7.0 (CPTI Working Group, 2004). The normal faulting focal mechanisms computed for the largest shocks agree with the tectonic regime of the region (Pondrelli et al., 2010; Scognamiglio et al., 2010; Herrmann et al., 2011). Seismological, geodetic, and geological data available for this earthquake sequence were extraordinary both in amount and quality, thus supplying fundamental insight into the source characteristics and complexity.
Through the years several investigators have mapped the numerous active faults of the region. These faults are mostly extensional and SW-dipping, but for many of them no consensus exists concerning state of activity and hierarchical level (i.e. the relevance for seismic hazard). Similarly to the mapped surface faults, all the different seismogenic sources proposed in the literature prior to the L'Aquila earthquake dip to the SW and display extensional kinematics; the geometric and dimensional relationships of each seismogenic source with the overlying surface faults, however, has been the object of a lively debate for at least a decade.
The identification of the surface expression of the fault responsible for the 6 April 2009 earthquake is controversial. There is general consensus that the upward prolongation of the subsurface rupture plane intersects the ground surface near the Paganica Fault, a poorly known tectonic feature having a subtle geomorphic expression (Bagnaia et al., 1989; Pace et al., 2006) referred to as an "uncertain or buried fault" in the digital version of the official geological maps of Italy (Servizio Geologico d'Italia, 2006).
The seismological evidence that aftershocks were confined below 2 km depth (Chiarabba et al., 2009; Chiaraluce et al., 2011) suggests that only minor slip took place along the shallower portion of the fault, which agrees with surface observations. The fact that the deep seismogenic source may have complex relationships with the surface Paganica Fault is also suggested by the relatively low dip angle of the rupture plane and by its projection in the foot-wall of the Paganica Fault itself, as seen in the seismological sections published by Chiarabba et al. (2009) and Chiaraluce et al. (2011).
The rupture area and slip distribution of the activated fault have been constrained by various investigators through modelling of GPS, SAR and seismological data. The subsurface rupture length varies in the range 12 to 19 km (Anzidei et al., 2009; Atzori et al., 2009; Cheloni et al., 2010; Trasatti et al., 2011). The maximum slip at depth was about 1 m with only a few centimeters of slip predicted on the shallower portion of the fault (e.g. Cirella et alii, 2009). In contrast, field reconnaissance of surface co-seismic ruptures showed only discontinuous ground cracks and surface faulting with few centimeters of ground displacement: the total length and the geometry of the mapped surface breaks also varied greatly according to the different investigators. Accordingly, the surface evidence has been described 1) as a discontinuous 2.5 to 6 km-long segment of the Paganica Fault (Emergeo Working Group, 2009; Cinti et al., 2011; Vittori et al., 2011), 2) as three discontinuous 10 km-long en-echelon segments of the Paganica Fault (Falcucci et al., 2009), 3) as three narrow discontinuous 13 km-long fracture zones (Boncio et al., 2010), 4) as three discontinuous 19 km-long sub-parallel splays of the Paganica-San Demetrio fault system (Galli et al., 2010).
The surface strains induced by the L'Aquila source appear to fit rather convincingly the majority of the recent geological and morphotectonic features of the region, suggesting that they are related with sustained slip over the fault plane that ruptured on 6 April. For instance, the largest coseismic subsidence revealed by InSAR analyses fits perfectly with the active depocenter of the L'Aquila Quaternary basin, and minor reactivations of surface faults were observed coincident with the steepest gradients of the region that was coseismically downthrown.
According to the current historical catalogue, over the past 7 centuries the region hit by the 2009 earthquake sequence and that located south-east of L'Aquila has been hit by three significant shocks:
- 1315, 3 December (M 5.6; from CPTI11), a rather obscure earthquake that is known to have generically damaged "the Aquila castles";
- 1461, 27 November (M 6.4; from CPTI11), similar in strength to the 2009 but difficult to locate precisely due to the small number of intensity data, possibly reflecting an overlap with the effects of the 1456 earthquake, on the northern slope of the Maiella Massif;
- 1762, 6 October (M 6.0; from CPTI11), that struck all villages around San Pio delle Camere.
The 1461 and 1762 damage distributions are rather similar to that of the 2009 earthquake but shifted towards the southeast by a few km. According to Di Bucci et al. (2011), overall the accuracy of the available locations does not allow any of these earthquakes to be assigned confidently to the 6 April or to nearby sources. On the other hand, based on re-analysis of historical sources and comparison of macroseismic intensity maps of the 1461, 1762 and 2009 earthquakes, Tertulliani et al. (2010) highlighted the similarity of the damaged areas and proposed that the two older events may have been generated by the same source of the 2009 earthquake. Alternatively, noting that if the previous hypothesis was correct it would imply a very short return time on the same source, shorter than any other in Italy, they proposed that one of the two older events may have been generated by a source anthitetic to the Paganica Source but located along the same stretch of the L'Aquila basin.
Palaeoseismological studies across different segments of the Paganica and San Demetrio Faults returned different results: on the one hand Galli et al. (2010; 2011) contended that the Paganica Fault ruptured during the 1461 and the 2 February 1703 (M 6.7) earthquakes, while on the other hand Cinti et al. (2011) recognised five paleo-events which include the 1461 but not the 1703 earthquake, with an average interevent time of about 500 years for the three youngest events. Based on the long-term fault scarp geomorphology, however, Cinti et al. (2011) also suggested that the Paganica Fault could occasionally generate larger earthquakes.
Cinti et al. (2011) calculate a late Pleistocene dip-slip rate for the Paganica Fault of 0.2-0.4 mm/y, consistent with 0.25-0.5 mm/y for the early Pleistocene. Using age and throw of individual events, they calculate a similar late Holocene average dip-slip rate of ca. 0.3-0.4 mm/y. This suggests that the portion of the Paganica Fault where the 2009 continuous surface faulting occurred has fairly a constant average slip release since late Pleistocene. Galli et al. (2010) maintain that during the past 0.5 My the slip of the Paganica Fault has not been uniformly distributed among the different fault splays, either in time or in space, and calculated that the most representative slip rate of the structure, over different time windows, was about 0.5 mm/y. Such long-term slip rates are comparable with a) the 0.3 mm/y estimate from ground-penetrating radar survey by Roberts et al. (2010), and b) post-24.8 kyr slip-rate higher than 0.24 mm/y derived from paleoseismic evidence by Boncio et al. (2010).
The geometric parameters of this Source were constrained following the numerous seismological, geodetic (GPS and DInSAR) and geological studies published following the 6 April 2009 earthquake. More in detail:
- the location of the source is constrained by hypocentral locations of the main shock and of the following aftershocks, and by modeling of the coseismic surface vertical deformation;
- the length is constrained by the inversion of GPS and DInSAR data (e.g. Anzidei et al. 2009; Atzori et al., 2009;
- the width was calculated using empirical relationships linking fault dimensions;
- the minimum and maximum depth are based on the location of the mainshock, and are in agreement with joint inversion of GPS and DInSAR data (Atzori et al., 2009), and with the cutoff of the seismicity (Chiarabba et al., 2009);
- the strike is based on joint inversion of GPS and DInSAR data (Atzori et al., 2009);
- dip and rake are based on seismological data;
- the slip rate is based on the offset of early Pleistocene lacustrine deposits (Cinti et al., 2011).
The inversion of GPS and DInSAR data constrained a source characterized by a minimum depth of 2-3 km, indicating that most of the dynamic slip during the 6 April 2009 earthquake was attained at depth. A similar results was obtained inverting the same dataset for estimating the variable slip distribution on the fault plane, with only few centimeters of slip predicted on the more surficial portion of the fault (Atzori et al., 2009; Cheloni et al., 2010; Walters et al., 2009). These models agree with the slip distribution obtained by non-linear joint inversion of GPS and strong motion data (Cirella et al., 2009). These results are also in agreement with field evidence of coseismic ground ruptures that showed only few centimeters of ground displacement along a 3 km long section of the Paganica Fault, but are apparently in contrast with preliminary paleoseismological results wich show the occurrence of larger displacement events. However, a portion of the difference in displacement may be explained by postseismic amplification of the throw due to the afterslip along the fault, as it was testified for the 2009 earthquake by re-measurements of the coseismic ground offsets (e.g. Boncio et al., 2010).
Comparing the coseismic ground deformation registered by DInSAR data with geological and geomorphological constrains of long-term tectonic surface deformation (e.g. distribution and age of outcropping sintectonic deposits, drainage pattern and behavior) it seems that the 2009 earthquake could be categorized as characteristic. However, the larger displacement events discovered in paleoseismological trenches along the Paganica Fault imply larger magnitude earthquakes respect to the 2009 event (i.e. a wider and larger seismogenic source and larger coseismic slip) and a non-characteristic behavior of the seismogenic source.
